### 1.6 Normalization

{%- set norm_params = normalization.get('pipeline_params', {}) -%}
{%- set norm_strat = norm_params.get('strategies', {}) -%}
{%- set norm_method = norm_strat.get('normalization_method', 'ROBUST_LOG_ONLY') | upper -%}
{%- set is_log = norm_strat.get('log_transform_active', False) %}
{%- set vsn_p = norm_params.get('vsn_parameters', {}) %}
{%- set auto_sel = norm_params.get('auto_selection', {}) %}

Systematic analytical variations—such as global concentration disparities, dilution effects, and instrument sensitivity drift—were mitigated to ensure downstream statistical fidelity.

{%- if norm_method == 'ROBUST_LOG_ONLY' %}
**Robust Log2 Transformation Only**
Sample-wise intensity correction was bypassed, and a robust Log2 transformation was applied to compress the dynamic range while preserving the original sample-wise scaling structure.

{%- elif norm_method == 'QUANTILE' %}
**Global Quantile Normalization**
A standalone global quantile normalization was applied. By imposing identical statistical distributions across all samples, it effectively mitigates complex, non-linear instrumental drifts.
{%- if is_log %} Prior to quantile alignment, a robust Log2 transformation was applied to stabilize variance.{%- endif %}

{%- elif norm_method == 'VSN' %}
**Variance Stabilizing Normalization (VSN)**
To address the heteroscedasticity inherent in mass spectrometry data, Variance Stabilizing Normalization (VSN) was applied. The internal optimization engine determined the maximum likelihood parameters for the variance stabilization curve, converging on a structural scale factor of **{{ "%0.3e" | format(vsn_p.get('vsn_scale', 0)) if vsn_p.get('vsn_scale') is number else 'N/A' }}** and a generalized baseline shift of **{{ "%0.3e" | format(vsn_p.get('vsn_shift', 0)) if vsn_p.get('vsn_shift') is number else 'N/A' }}**. This transformation shifts the data into a generalized logarithmic space (glog) optimized for multivariate modeling.

{%- else %}
**Global Intensity Correction (Sample-wise)**
To mitigate analytical baseline fluctuations across the injection sequence, the data matrix underwent sample-wise normalization utilizing the **{{ norm_method }}** algorithm.
{%- if is_log %} Following sample calibration, a robust Log2 transformation was applied to stabilize variance.{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}

{%- if auto_sel %}
**Automatic Normalization Selection**
The normalization strategy was selected automatically from predefined log-only, sample-wise normalization, distribution-alignment, and variance-stabilizing candidates. Candidate methods were compared using four label-independent composite scores: QC precision change, RLE distributional-alignment change, MA intensity-bias change, and local sample-structure preservation. The selected strategy was **{{ auto_sel.get('selected_method', norm_method) }}**
{%- if auto_sel.get('selected_score') is number %} with an overall score of **{{ auto_sel.get('selected_score') | round(3) }}**{%- endif %}
.
{%- endif %}

**Global Distribution Fidelity**
{%- set norm_qa = norm_params.get('quality_metrics', {}) -%}
{%- set sim_qc = norm_qa.get('JSD', {}).get('QC', {}) -%}
{%- set sim_sam = norm_qa.get('JSD', {}).get('Sample', {}) %}

The structural integrity of the Pooled QC samples remained consistent, yielding a Before vs After JSD score of **{{ sim_qc.get('Before vs After', 'N/A') | round(3) if sim_qc.get('Before vs After') is number else sim_qc.get('Before vs After', 'N/A') }}**. Concurrently, actual biological samples exhibited a controlled density evolution with a JSD score of **{{ sim_sam.get('Before vs After', 'N/A') | round(3) if sim_sam.get('Before vs After') is number else sim_sam.get('Before vs After', 'N/A') }}**.

